# FreshGuard dashboard — copy to .env.local (git-ignored) or .env and adjust.
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL — base URL of the FreshGuard API, when it is not same-origin.
#
# Leave this UNSET in the ordinary case. The dev server and `vite preview` both
# proxy /api, /healthz and /readyz to the backend, so the page and the API share
# one origin and CORS never applies. Setting it here is only for a genuinely
# separate API host, and it bypasses that proxy.
#
# Do not point it at 127.0.0.1 to make the dev server work: it already works
# same-origin, and from a phone on the LAN 127.0.0.1 resolves to the phone, so
# every request fails while the same build works on the machine serving it.
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
#
# VITE_ADMIN_TOKEN — bearer token for administrator writes.
#
# The read API, the CSV export and the live SSE feed need no credential. Saving
# thresholds does: `PUT /api/v1/devices/:dev/thresholds` requires
# `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, and the service was started with the matching
# secret (see the backend's own env file).
#
# Leave this UNSET for a read-only dashboard. Every panel works without it; the
# only thing that stops is saving limits, and the panel then says so explicitly
# rather than reporting a generic failure.
#
# This is a build-time value baked into the bundle by Vite, and VITE_-prefixed
# variables are readable by anything the page serves. Treat it as a shared
# operator credential for an internal tool, not as a per-user secret: it does not
# identify who saved a change, which is why the panel asks for a name separately
# and the backend records that.
# VITE_ADMIN_TOKEN=
